## The Two Borrow Rules

At any given time, you may have:
1. **Many read borrows (&)** — OR —
2. **One write borrow (&mut)**

**Never both at the same time.**

### Rule 1: Many & Allowed

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let data = Vec[Int].new();
  data.push(1);
  let r1 = &data;
  let r2 = &data;
  let r3 = &data;  // Three read borrows — perfectly fine!
  io.println(r1.len());
  io.println(r2.len());
  io.println(r3.len());
  return data.len() as Int;
}
```

### Rule 2: One &mut, No Others

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var data = Vec[Int].new();
  let r = &mut data;  // Exclusive write access
  r.push(10);
  // No other borrows allowed while r is live
  return data.len() as Int;
}
```

### Rule Violations Are Compile-Time Errors

```xiom
// This WON'T COMPILE:
var data = Vec[Int].new();
let r1 = &data;
let r2 = &mut data;  // ERROR: can't borrow mutably while & exists
r2.push(10);
```

### These Rules Prevent Real Bugs

- **Data races**: Two threads can't write simultaneously
- **Iterator invalidation**: Can't modify a collection while iterating
- **Dangling pointers**: References can't outlive their data

